  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 248 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nA cold front will linger over central MO for the rest of the \nweek and bring intermittent rain chances to that area. Most of\nthe area will remain dry and hot for the rest of the week due to\na high pressure system that will situate itself over the\nsoutheast CONUS. \n\nThere may be a shortwave on Tuesday that brings rain a bit \nfurther south (30-60%), that's where the Marginal risk (1/5) for\nsevere weather comes in. Right now, wind seems to be the main \nthreat with any severe storms and those storms should stay \nconfined to areas along and north of I-44. We'll have plenty of\nlift and instability due to the front and deep-layer shear will\nbe adequate near 30 knots. Though, the better environment for\nsevere weather will likely be further north, outside of our \nCWA. \n\nTowards the end of the work week is when the high pressure \nsystem begins to push further east and when rain chances attempt\nto become more widespread. There has already been a decrease in\nrain chances later this week which may indicate that the ridge \nsticks around a bit longer than anticipated. Rain chances \nbecoming more widespread will be determined by the behavior of \nthe ridge. If the ridge stays put, it'll be more difficult to \nget rain any further south than central MO. Highs will be in the\n90s this week with temperatures lowering into the 80s for next\nweekend.",
